# Plan: GitHub Integration Setup (Beginner)

## Context
The user is an absolute beginner who wants to set up GitHub integration before starting a new Rust/Go/C++ project. They already have a GitHub account. Currently, neither `git` nor the `gh` CLI are installed on this Linux machine. The goal is to go from zero to a fully connected local project with a remote GitHub repository.

---

## Steps

### 1. Install git
```bash
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y git
```

### 2. Install gh (GitHub CLI)
The recommended way on Ubuntu/Debian:
```bash
sudo apt install -y curl
curl -fsSL https://cli.github.com/packages/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg | sudo dd of=/usr/share/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg
echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg] https://cli.github.com/packages stable main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/github-cli.list > /dev/null
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y gh
```

### 3. Configure git identity
Set the name and email that will appear on every commit:
```bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your@email.com"
```
We will prompt the user for these values before running.

### 4. Authenticate with GitHub via gh
```bash
gh auth login
```
This opens an interactive flow: browser-based OAuth. Walk the user through choosing:
- GitHub.com
- HTTPS protocol
- Login with a web browser

### 5. Create the project directory and initialize git
```bash
mkdir ~/my-project
cd ~/my-project
git init
```
Ask the user for the project name before running.

### 6. Add a .gitignore appropriate for Rust/Go/C++
Create `/home/farm/my-project/.gitignore` with common patterns:
- Rust: `target/`, `Cargo.lock` (for libraries), `*.rs.bk`
- Go: `*.exe`, `*.test`, `*.out`, `vendor/`
- C++: `*.o`, `*.a`, `*.so`, `*.out`, `build/`, `cmake-build-*/`

We'll create a combined file since the language isn't pinned yet.

### 7. Create a GitHub repository and link it
```bash
gh repo create my-project --public --source=. --remote=origin --push
```
This single command:
- Creates the repo on GitHub
- Sets it as the `origin` remote
- Pushes the initial commit

Alternatively use `--private` if the user wants a private repo.

### 8. Verify
```bash
git remote -v        # confirm origin is set
gh repo view --web   # open the repo in the browser
```

---

## Files to create/modify
- `/home/farm/<project-name>/.gitignore` — new file, Rust/Go/C++ patterns
- `/home/farm/<project-name>/` — new project directory

## Questions still needed from user (during execution)
- Preferred project name
- Public or private repository
- Their git user.name and user.email (for git config)

